  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 1 card dq reg: grade 6.1: "How do the charges of ions decide the formula and the name of an ionic compound?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 3 s03-dq: grade 6.1: "How do the charges of ions decide the formula and the name of an ionic compound?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 6 s06-rule: grade 7.1: "Metal atoms lose 1, 2 or 3 valence electrons. Nonmetal atoms gain electrons to reach eight. A monatomic anion name ends in -ide."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 8 s08-rule: grade 6.9: "The total charge of an ionic compound is zero. The formula shows the smallest whole-number ratio of ions."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 10 s10-rule: grade 5.8: "Crisscross is a shortcut, not the final formula. Always reduce the ratio, then check the total charge."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 11 s11-rule: grade 5.4: "Name the cation first, then the anion root plus -ide. Common names omit prefixes because the ion charges determine the ratio."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 12 s12-c4: grade 6: "The copper ion's charge determines the formula. The Roman numeral records that charge in the name."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 16 s16-rule: grade 7: "Treat a polyatomic ion as one unit. Never change the subscripts inside a polyatomic ion."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 21 card prompt reg: grade 6: "Write the formula of aluminum oxide from Al 3+ and O 2−. Explain why the ratio is correct."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 21 s21-sample: grade 5.5: "The formula is Al 2 O 3. Two aluminum ions provide 6+, and three oxide ions provide 6−. The total charge equals zero."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 22 card prompt reg: grade 6.7: "Iron forms two different chlorides, FeCl 2 and FeCl 3. Name both compounds and explain how you determined each Roman numeral."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 22 s22-sample: grade 5.7: "FeCl 2 is iron(II) chloride. FeCl 3 is iron(III) chloride. Each chloride ion is 1−, so iron balances them."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 23 card prompt reg: grade 7.4: "Some antacids contain aluminum hydroxide. Write its formula. Explain why it needs parentheses but NaOH does not."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 23 s23-sample: grade 5.4: "The formula is Al(OH) 3. Aluminum needs three hydroxide ions, so they go in parentheses. NaOH has one."
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 24 card prompt reg dq: grade 6.1: "How do the charges of ions decide the formula and the name of an ionic compound?"
  register (rule/prompt/sample) slide 24 s24-sample: grade 5.5: "The total charge must be zero, so ion charges determine the ratio. Copper(I) gives Cu 2 O; copper(II) gives CuO."
